Limitations and assumptions
===========================

What PTForge cannot do, and which parts are built from the documentation without a confirmed run in every Packet Tracer version.

Not possible through the API
----------------------------

* Clusters: The Packet Tracer API only clusters the current mouse selection
* Filled rectangles and shapes: Only lines, circles and notes can be drawn. Rectangles are drawn as four lines
* Building a .pts package: Packages are encrypted by Packet Tracer
* Selecting or dragging with the mouse: Mouse only features are out of scope

Tested how
----------

The test suite (npm test) runs the whole extension against a mock of the Packet Tracer IPC API. It checks the commands sent, the API calls made, argument handling, errors, every example and the release bundle. It cannot prove that Packet Tracer itself accepts every call.

API method names and arguments come from the official Cisco Packet Tracer IPC documentation.

Checked against the official API
------------------------------

Every IPC method named in this project exists in the Cisco Packet Tracer
Extensions API 8.1.0 reference (https://tutorials.ptnetacad.net/help/default/IpcAPI/annotated.html).
The device type numbers, the cable constants and the five command statuses are the
values printed in that reference, not guesses.

Seven methods that Packet Tracer really has are not in the 8.1.0 class reference:
registerEvent, unregisterEvent, ipc.network, ipc.commandLog, ipc.systemFileManager
makeDirectory, simulationPanel play and the editor bridge receiveOutput. They are
called behind typeof checks (callIfExists), so a build without them degrades to a
clear error instead of crashing. If a future release documents them, the calls move
out of this list.

Enter Command
-------------

enterCommand returns a pair of status and output. The status values are counted, not
guessed: 0 ok, 1 ambiguous, 2 invalid, 3 incomplete, 4 not implemented. PTForge
reads pairs, arrays, plain numbers and plain strings, because different versions
hand the value back in different shapes.

Lines are trimmed before they are sent. A builder that indents its output (interface
blocks start with a space) would otherwise send " login local" to the parser, which
some versions answer with incomplete or invalid.

IPv6 access lists
-----------------

The API has Aclv6Process next to AclProcess, so IPv6 lists exist as data. Packet
Tracer still simulates IPv6 with gaps: filters are evaluated for PDUs it simulates
over IPv6, and a few PDU types skip the filter. A filter that does nothing in a lab
is usually this, not a wrong command.

Device type numbers
-------------------

DeviceType numbers change between Packet Tracer releases. A model that lands on the
wrong kind of device is the first sign that the build you run no longer matches the
8.1.0 numbers. getDeviceType(name) after addDevice shows what Packet Tracer stored.

Assumptions to confirm in Packet Tracer
---------------------------------------

* Show commands: CLI output is returned by enterCommand. Some versions return an empty string
* 3650 trunks: switchport trunk encapsulation dot1q may be rejected. The rejection is reported and the rest applies
* HTTPS: The server process is named HttpsServer
* Wireless: WirelessServer / WirelessCommon method names come from the docs
* Simulation filters: Protocol names such as ICMP must match the Packet Tracer filter list
* DHCP relay: setDhcpRelay sends ip helper-address and needs a router or layer 3 switch
* Inspection processes: VlanManager, StpMain, Vtp, MacSwitch, OspfMain, EigrpMain process names follow the naming used by server processes. Both forms with and without Process are tried
* Enum values: modeCode fields are raw numbers because the documentation does not list enum values
* Owner device lookup: getOwnerDevice may be missing in older versions. Functions that use it fall back gracefully

Please report results for your Packet Tracer version in the issues.
